1
00:00:04,780 --> 00:00:08,830
Good to have you here. Welcome back to
Steadfast Men.

2
00:00:09,240 --> 00:00:13,610
This is Lesson Three. It's called The Four
Marks.

3
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:15,370
Here's how this goes.

4
00:00:15,960 --> 00:00:17,770
The word under all of it.

5
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:18,830
Mark.

6
00:00:19,540 --> 00:00:24,670
Then four things a man does, said the way
I'd say them at a kitchen table.

7
00:00:25,180 --> 00:00:28,130
Then the last of those four, held longer.

8
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:30,130
Who the work is for.

9
00:00:30,740 --> 00:00:35,810
Then a shut door, a tired morning, and a
harvest on his clock.

10
00:00:36,460 --> 00:00:40,910
Then your life as it actually is, and the
move that follows.

11
00:00:41,960 --> 00:00:44,750
In the lesson before this, I said it
straight.

12
00:00:45,140 --> 00:00:49,830
You are not crazy. Those orders you were
given fight each other.

13
00:00:50,260 --> 00:00:52,930
A plain answer is a good thing to want.

14
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:58,250
You heard the four at the close, and I
promised the extra time to the last one.

15
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:00,350
I also gave you a move.

16
00:01:00,880 --> 00:01:06,350
Say the orders to one man, name the one
you've been living, and trade sentences
with

17
00:01:06,220 --> 00:01:06,510
him.

18
00:01:07,020 --> 00:01:08,410
So did you do it?

19
00:01:08,920 --> 00:01:12,390
If it only got half done, the rest is
still yours.

20
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:15,650
If the coffee came from your kitchen, I
get it.

21
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:17,330
You've met church coffee.

22
00:01:17,860 --> 00:01:19,990
If you hang back, I did too.

23
00:01:20,460 --> 00:01:22,810
I sat in the back for about ten years.

24
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:24,370
I know the excuse.

25
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:29,370
New to this? Start here. You can catch the
earlier lessons after.

26
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:32,170
You and I go one lesson at a time.

27
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,490
I will not hand you twelve fixes before
you reach your car.

28
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:40,290
You've had those lists. I've written them.

29
00:01:40,720 --> 00:01:44,530
I've lost most of them. A list feels great
for about a day.

30
00:01:44,980 --> 00:01:49,990
Then an ordinary Tuesday shows up, and the
list is on the counter, under the mail.

31
00:01:50,480 --> 00:01:52,050
I found one of mine the other day.

32
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:55,110
Seven things. I'd done one of them.

33
00:01:55,480 --> 00:01:57,810
And the one I'd done was buy stamps.

34
00:01:58,220 --> 00:02:01,670
I didn't even mail anything. I just owned
stamps.

35
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:04,150
So no list.

36
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:09,390
Four handles. One of them, held long
enough that it gets into your hands.

37
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:13,330
Miss the word, and the four turn into a
poster.

38
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:17,170
You've got enough posters. The word is
mark.

39
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:21,990
There's a doorframe in our house nobody's
allowed to paint.

40
00:02:22,440 --> 00:02:25,270
The pantry door. The inside edge.

41
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:30,610
Every birthday since my daughter could
stand, she backs up against that wood.

42
00:02:31,140 --> 00:02:34,310
I lay a book flat on her head and make a
pencil line.

43
00:02:34,660 --> 00:02:38,290
Her name. The date. A little crooked some
years.

44
00:02:39,340 --> 00:02:41,530
I never see her grow on a Tuesday.

45
00:02:41,860 --> 00:02:42,750
Not once.

46
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:48,310
I see her every single day, and every
single day she looks like yesterday.

47
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:52,470
Then I open the pantry for the peanut
butter, and there it is.

48
00:02:53,060 --> 00:02:55,590
Six inches I never watched happen.

49
00:02:56,080 --> 00:03:01,330
Her grandparents see it. They come twice a
year and say, look how tall she's gotten.

50
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:03,590
And I want to say, has she?

51
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:05,450
I've been standing right here.

52
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:08,750
That's how a mark works on a man too.

53
00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:11,710
The people closest to it notice last.

54
00:03:12,280 --> 00:03:15,650
One year there's a note by the line, in
her handwriting.

55
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:18,590
It says, Dad pushed the book down.

56
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:22,710
I did not push the book down. She was on
her tiptoes.

57
00:03:24,060 --> 00:03:28,950
A mark is what's left on a man by what he
does over and over.

58
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:32,510
What you feel on a good morning is not a
mark.

59
00:03:32,980 --> 00:03:35,510
A title from a banquet is not a mark.

60
00:03:36,060 --> 00:03:40,570
A man who's short with his kids at supper
doesn't feel a mark being made.

61
00:03:40,920 --> 00:03:42,910
He calls it one bad night.

62
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:46,530
Then it's a long string of one bad nights.

63
00:03:46,960 --> 00:03:49,170
His kids are the ones holding the pencil.

64
00:03:49,540 --> 00:03:50,870
It cuts both ways.

65
00:03:52,740 --> 00:03:55,550
So a real man has marks.

66
00:03:56,260 --> 00:03:58,030
Four I keep coming back to.

67
00:03:58,620 --> 00:04:03,510
Do them enough and they quit being a mood,
and they hold up on an ordinary day.

68
00:04:04,060 --> 00:04:06,510
I've laid them over my own life for years.

69
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:10,390
They're not always kind to me. They've
always been right.

70
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:13,950
Here's how I'd say them across a kitchen
table.

71
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:18,550
A real man doesn't sit on his hands when
something's his to do.

72
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:22,770
He picks up what's his, even when somebody
else dropped it.

73
00:04:23,140 --> 00:04:29,330
He goes first, scared or not. And he's
working for God's pay, not the room's.

74
00:04:30,980 --> 00:04:31,910
Four marks.

75
00:04:32,380 --> 00:04:36,470
Every one of them is something you do, on
a day you don't want to.

76
00:04:37,040 --> 00:04:39,610
Agreeing with me will not put one on you.

77
00:04:40,660 --> 00:04:45,250
The first three each get a lesson of their
own, later, taken one by one.

78
00:04:45,680 --> 00:04:50,350
I won't crack all four open here and send
you home with sermons and no practice.

79
00:04:50,800 --> 00:04:53,890
This lesson stays with the one a man
skips.

80
00:04:54,540 --> 00:04:55,690
Last on the list.

81
00:04:56,160 --> 00:05:00,850
Easy to read it like fine print, the lines
you skim to reach a signature.

82
00:05:01,920 --> 00:05:04,370
Stay with me. This part surprised me.

83
00:05:04,920 --> 00:05:09,650
A decent man can keep the first three a
long time and leave God out.

84
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:13,850
He moves. He carries the load. He goes
first.

85
00:05:14,240 --> 00:05:18,930
I respect that. The fourth mark shows who
he is actually working for.

86
00:05:19,440 --> 00:05:22,250
I had not really asked. Maybe you haven't
either.

87
00:05:24,100 --> 00:05:26,450
Hebrews chapter eleven, verse six.

88
00:05:27,120 --> 00:05:32,370
"And without faith it is impossible to
please God, because anyone who comes to
him

89
00:05:32,380 --> 00:05:37,230
must believe that he exists and that he
rewards those who earnestly seek him."

90
00:05:38,660 --> 00:05:41,370
Two things in that verse. That he is.

91
00:05:41,880 --> 00:05:43,610
And that he rewards.

92
00:05:44,660 --> 00:05:46,710
You'd sign the first one without blinking.

93
00:05:47,080 --> 00:05:48,750
God exists. Sure.

94
00:05:49,340 --> 00:05:51,550
Reward sounds a little mercenary.

95
00:05:51,840 --> 00:05:53,550
Like you're only in it for the paycheck.

96
00:05:53,880 --> 00:05:57,070
Like the kid who asks what he gets before
he'll take out the trash.

97
00:05:57,620 --> 00:06:02,790
The verse doesn't blush. A man who comes
to God has to believe God pays.

98
00:06:03,140 --> 00:06:03,870
Not maybe.

99
00:06:04,360 --> 00:06:04,970
Must.

100
00:06:06,020 --> 00:06:10,850
If you believe God pays, you can do a
costly thing, and the room doesn't have to

101
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:11,370
settle it.

102
00:06:12,440 --> 00:06:15,010
You work for some kind of pay. So do I.

103
00:06:15,480 --> 00:06:17,890
Nobody repeats the hard thing for nothing.

104
00:06:18,280 --> 00:06:21,110
The question is who you expect the pay
from.

105
00:06:21,620 --> 00:06:24,670
If the answer isn't God, you go collect
somewhere else.

106
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,370
The man you report to. A good review.

107
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:30,150
The group text. A nod from family.

108
00:06:30,620 --> 00:06:33,490
None of that is evil. Thanks is a gift.

109
00:06:34,020 --> 00:06:39,510
Thanks makes a lousy wage. When thanks is
the wage, you quit the day it stops.

110
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:43,850
And it stops. It always stops. People get
busy.

111
00:06:44,160 --> 00:06:47,670
They get used to you. The good you do
turns into furniture.

112
00:06:48,020 --> 00:06:49,470
Nobody thanks the couch.

113
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:52,510
A man needs a pay that doesn't run out.

114
00:06:54,360 --> 00:06:55,650
I'll tell on myself.

115
00:06:56,260 --> 00:06:58,550
My daughter needed a desk a while back.

116
00:06:58,860 --> 00:07:04,330
The flat-pack kind. About a thousand
pieces, and one tiny wrench.

117
00:07:04,980 --> 00:07:06,710
I built it after she went to bed.

118
00:07:07,120 --> 00:07:09,330
Took me till almost one in the morning.

119
00:07:09,820 --> 00:07:12,390
Put a lamp on it. Squared up the chair.

120
00:07:12,780 --> 00:07:17,230
Hid the leftover screws, because there are
always leftover screws.

121
00:07:18,300 --> 00:07:21,070
Next morning I'm in the kitchen, hovering.

122
00:07:21,480 --> 00:07:25,390
Coffee in my hand. Standing right where I
can see her door.

123
00:07:26,080 --> 00:07:26,650
Waiting.

124
00:07:27,460 --> 00:07:29,330
I refilled that coffee twice.

125
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:33,910
I didn't want the coffee. I wanted a
reason to be standing there.

126
00:07:34,620 --> 00:07:38,350
My wife walked through and read the whole
thing in about a second.

127
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:41,470
She patted my arm. Didn't say a word.

128
00:07:42,160 --> 00:07:47,230
Then my daughter comes out, walks straight
past the desk, and asks if we have any

129
00:07:47,160 --> 00:07:48,590
more of the good cereal.

130
00:07:49,640 --> 00:07:51,190
I felt this little drop.

131
00:07:51,840 --> 00:07:55,170
Grown man. Pouting over particleboard.

132
00:07:55,760 --> 00:07:57,850
I said it out loud, to nobody.

133
00:07:58,520 --> 00:08:01,250
Huh, looks like somebody's got a new desk.

134
00:08:02,600 --> 00:08:04,470
She did thank me later that day.

135
00:08:05,140 --> 00:08:06,830
I hadn't built that desk for her.

136
00:08:07,220 --> 00:08:08,170
Not all the way.

137
00:08:08,720 --> 00:08:11,410
Part of me built it for the moment she'd
see it.

138
00:08:11,920 --> 00:08:13,270
The moment came late.

139
00:08:13,780 --> 00:08:15,090
I wanted a refund.

140
00:08:16,160 --> 00:08:20,930
Have you hovered like that? Fixed a thing,
then hung around until somebody found it?

141
00:08:22,220 --> 00:08:25,390
Yeah. Same. That's the wrong pay.

142
00:08:25,960 --> 00:08:28,410
It looks exactly like a good dad.

143
00:08:28,820 --> 00:08:30,670
It runs out a lot faster.

144
00:08:32,540 --> 00:08:35,110
Lonnie sat in a group with me for years.

145
00:08:35,580 --> 00:08:38,370
He was not a talker. Big hands.

146
00:08:38,680 --> 00:08:43,610
Quiet voice. The kind of man who fixes
your mower and won't let you pay him.

147
00:08:44,120 --> 00:08:49,790
Same corner every time. About four
sentences a night, and every one of them
was

148
00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:50,650
worth the wait.

149
00:08:51,720 --> 00:08:53,250
Lonnie's dad got sick.

150
00:08:53,740 --> 00:08:55,890
Kidney trouble. Dialysis.

151
00:08:56,480 --> 00:08:58,750
Monday, Wednesday, Friday.

152
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:00,170
Six in the morning.

153
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:01,570
Lonnie drove him.

154
00:09:02,060 --> 00:09:05,510
Every one of those mornings, a little over
four years.

155
00:09:06,060 --> 00:09:08,290
Four hours in the chair each time.

156
00:09:08,720 --> 00:09:12,210
Then Lonnie went to work and made the
hours up on the back end.

157
00:09:12,720 --> 00:09:15,410
His wife held the house together on those
mornings.

158
00:09:15,720 --> 00:09:17,070
He'd tell you that first.

159
00:09:18,140 --> 00:09:22,090
At first, people noticed. Church asked
about his dad.

160
00:09:22,460 --> 00:09:25,470
Somebody brought a casserole. A good one,
he said.

161
00:09:25,780 --> 00:09:26,850
Chicken and rice.

162
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:29,570
Two friends told him he was a good son.

163
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,410
That helps. I won't pretend it doesn't.

164
00:09:32,980 --> 00:09:35,490
By the second year, nobody asked.

165
00:09:35,860 --> 00:09:39,110
They hadn't turned cold. It had stopped
being news.

166
00:09:40,160 --> 00:09:43,390
His dad was never an easy man. The route.

167
00:09:43,680 --> 00:09:46,550
The radio. The turn into the clinic lot.

168
00:09:46,900 --> 00:09:50,830
Four years of that, and I don't think he
heard thank you twice.

169
00:09:51,280 --> 00:09:55,570
Fair to his dad, though. Sick, scared, and
hooked to a machine.

170
00:09:56,060 --> 00:09:57,630
I'd complain about the turn too.

171
00:09:58,500 --> 00:10:01,490
One night Lonnie said, it's not the
driving.

172
00:10:01,820 --> 00:10:03,110
I can do the driving.

173
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:05,930
I'm just the only one who knows I'm doing
it.

174
00:10:07,280 --> 00:10:09,250
Then he told what happened next.

175
00:10:09,880 --> 00:10:12,330
One of those mornings he got to the clinic
early.

176
00:10:12,760 --> 00:10:17,070
The doors weren't open yet. His dad was
dozing in the passenger seat.

177
00:10:17,400 --> 00:10:20,610
Engine running. Heater on. Still dark.

178
00:10:21,180 --> 00:10:26,030
A nurse sat in her car two spots over,
eating a breakfast sandwich, waiting on
the

179
00:10:25,960 --> 00:10:26,870
same doors.

180
00:10:27,920 --> 00:10:30,390
Lonnie said he started talking to God.

181
00:10:30,960 --> 00:10:32,110
Not a pretty prayer.

182
00:10:32,580 --> 00:10:33,130
He said,

183
00:10:33,620 --> 00:10:35,850
Lord, nobody knows I'm here.

184
00:10:36,660 --> 00:10:39,370
Right in the middle of that sentence, it
hit him.

185
00:10:39,880 --> 00:10:40,930
That's not true.

186
00:10:41,500 --> 00:10:42,210
One does.

187
00:10:43,580 --> 00:10:46,130
He sat there till the lights came on
inside.

188
00:10:46,460 --> 00:10:49,150
Didn't say anything else. He just knew it.

189
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:53,190
He told it in maybe six sentences, then
went quiet.

190
00:10:54,240 --> 00:10:55,870
The mornings didn't get shorter.

191
00:10:56,180 --> 00:10:59,970
His dad didn't get sweeter. Church never
threw him a dinner.

192
00:11:00,320 --> 00:11:03,610
He quit driving for a thank you he was
never gonna get.

193
00:11:03,960 --> 00:11:07,610
He started driving for a Father who was
already watching.

194
00:11:08,680 --> 00:11:10,710
His dad passed a while after that.

195
00:11:11,160 --> 00:11:14,430
At the service, nobody said a word about
the driving.

196
00:11:14,700 --> 00:11:16,050
Not one person.

197
00:11:16,600 --> 00:11:19,190
I asked him later. I figured it would
sting.

198
00:11:19,680 --> 00:11:23,850
He shrugged. He said, Wes, I didn't need
them to say it.

199
00:11:24,080 --> 00:11:25,390
It's already written down.

200
00:11:26,740 --> 00:11:29,270
Lonnie didn't get that mark by feeling
holy.

201
00:11:29,680 --> 00:11:33,170
Most of those mornings he felt tired, and
a little mad.

202
00:11:33,680 --> 00:11:38,090
He got it by pulling into that lot
something like six hundred times.

203
00:11:38,580 --> 00:11:39,710
That's a mark.

204
00:11:40,900 --> 00:11:43,170
Matthew chapter six, verse six.

205
00:11:43,840 --> 00:11:49,410
"But when you pray, go into your room,
close the door and pray to your Father,
who

206
00:11:49,320 --> 00:11:50,290
is unseen.

207
00:11:50,780 --> 00:11:54,670
Then your Father, who sees what is done in
secret, will reward you."

208
00:11:56,160 --> 00:11:57,390
Close the door.

209
00:11:58,060 --> 00:12:00,570
Jesus assumes you've got a life behind a
door.

210
00:12:00,980 --> 00:12:02,710
A part nobody claps for.

211
00:12:03,180 --> 00:12:05,290
He doesn't treat that part as second
string.

212
00:12:05,620 --> 00:12:08,170
He says that's where the Father is paying
attention.

213
00:12:08,560 --> 00:12:11,770
Your Father sees. Your Father rewards.

214
00:12:12,300 --> 00:12:14,210
Same verse. Same breath.

215
00:12:15,280 --> 00:12:17,530
That door is not a hiding place.

216
00:12:18,120 --> 00:12:21,270
Last lesson, hiding was what you did with
the wrong.

217
00:12:21,580 --> 00:12:23,990
You shut a door so the wrong stayed
covered.

218
00:12:24,380 --> 00:12:29,310
This is the other door. You shut it on the
good you did, so the only one who pays

219
00:12:29,220 --> 00:12:30,150
you is him.

220
00:12:31,380 --> 00:12:34,950
Most of what makes a man happens behind
some door.

221
00:12:35,600 --> 00:12:37,170
A kitchen at five thirty.

222
00:12:37,760 --> 00:12:41,810
The drive home, when you pick which
version of you is going to walk in.

223
00:12:42,380 --> 00:12:45,310
The apology nobody else knew you owed.

224
00:12:45,820 --> 00:12:48,970
The text you sent a man who's been quiet
too long.

225
00:12:49,460 --> 00:12:51,170
The screen you left shut.

226
00:12:52,260 --> 00:12:53,710
I'm going to ask you something.

227
00:12:54,160 --> 00:12:55,630
Keep the answer to yourself.

228
00:12:56,320 --> 00:12:59,650
Did you do something good lately that
nobody knows about?

229
00:13:00,140 --> 00:13:02,970
Keep it quiet. Saying it spends it.

230
00:13:04,320 --> 00:13:06,530
That quiet is the fourth mark.

231
00:13:07,020 --> 00:13:08,870
You might be thinking it wasn't much.

232
00:13:09,260 --> 00:13:12,610
It doesn't have to be big. It has to be
his.

233
00:13:13,160 --> 00:13:16,530
Behind the door is not the leftover part
of your life.

234
00:13:16,880 --> 00:13:18,750
It may be the realest part.

235
00:13:19,200 --> 00:13:22,630
Your Father has been watching that part
the whole time.

236
00:13:23,660 --> 00:13:25,170
I'll be honest about mine.

237
00:13:25,640 --> 00:13:29,710
Ask me which of the four is thinnest on
me, and it's this one.

238
00:13:30,180 --> 00:13:31,310
I'll do the good thing.

239
00:13:31,780 --> 00:13:33,850
I'm pretty reliable about the doing.

240
00:13:34,500 --> 00:13:36,370
Then I go looking for the receipt.

241
00:13:36,960 --> 00:13:39,170
I mention it sideways. I'll say,

242
00:13:39,620 --> 00:13:43,610
man, long day, had to run over and jump
Jim's battery.

243
00:13:44,100 --> 00:13:44,590
True.

244
00:13:45,080 --> 00:13:47,430
I didn't need to say it. I was fishing.

245
00:13:47,900 --> 00:13:50,490
Worst part, I usually catch something.

246
00:13:50,960 --> 00:13:53,970
Somebody says, man, that was good of you.

247
00:13:54,540 --> 00:13:56,890
I feel great for about ten minutes.

248
00:13:57,440 --> 00:14:00,490
My wife has a look for it. Not a mean
look.

249
00:14:00,820 --> 00:14:04,810
The look you give a dog who brings you the
same stick for the fourth time.

250
00:14:05,880 --> 00:14:10,610
I've done it at church. Stacked chairs a
little louder than the job required.

251
00:14:10,980 --> 00:14:14,390
Clang. Anybody see this? Clang.

252
00:14:14,960 --> 00:14:18,430
I trade what the Father was keeping for a
nod over supper.

253
00:14:18,900 --> 00:14:19,890
Bad trade.

254
00:14:20,380 --> 00:14:21,890
I make it all the time.

255
00:14:22,380 --> 00:14:23,270
Maybe that's you.

256
00:14:23,760 --> 00:14:26,230
Maybe another of the four is the thin one.

257
00:14:26,660 --> 00:14:30,030
I went first with mine, so yours has a
place to land.

258
00:14:31,880 --> 00:14:35,150
Galatians chapter six, verse nine.

259
00:14:35,140 --> 00:14:40,210
"Let us not become weary in doing good,
for at the proper time we will reap a

260
00:14:40,160 --> 00:14:42,210
harvest if we do not give up."

261
00:14:43,640 --> 00:14:46,630
Paul says weary like he's already met you.

262
00:14:47,120 --> 00:14:50,370
Doing good gets old. The same drive.

263
00:14:50,700 --> 00:14:56,070
The same dishes. The same prayer, for the
same kid, who doesn't seem to be

264
00:14:56,020 --> 00:14:56,630
listening.

265
00:14:57,720 --> 00:15:00,230
He is not scolding you for being tired.

266
00:15:00,540 --> 00:15:02,790
Tired is not the end of the story.

267
00:15:03,440 --> 00:15:07,870
Notice what he leaves out. He never says
you will see the harvest right away.

268
00:15:08,240 --> 00:15:12,070
He says, at the proper time. His time.

269
00:15:12,460 --> 00:15:14,430
Sometimes not in this life at all.

270
00:15:15,460 --> 00:15:18,470
I've got a prayer like that. Same man in
my family.

271
00:15:18,840 --> 00:15:21,510
Same request. A long time now.

272
00:15:22,040 --> 00:15:25,370
Some mornings I say it and it feels like
I'm talking to the ceiling.

273
00:15:25,880 --> 00:15:26,850
I say it anyway.

274
00:15:27,440 --> 00:15:29,330
I don't say it because I feel holy.

275
00:15:29,860 --> 00:15:33,010
I say it because the proper time is his to
pick.

276
00:15:34,040 --> 00:15:36,270
It's that pencil line on the pantry door.

277
00:15:36,700 --> 00:15:39,210
Nothing shows until somebody opens it.

278
00:15:40,280 --> 00:15:41,870
He names one condition.

279
00:15:42,320 --> 00:15:43,490
You don't quit.

280
00:15:44,040 --> 00:15:48,290
He does not require a perfect record, and
he does not require a feeling.

281
00:15:48,780 --> 00:15:51,390
That fourth mark holds the other three up.

282
00:15:51,900 --> 00:15:55,290
Moving when you'd rather wait. Picking up
what's yours.

283
00:15:55,680 --> 00:15:58,030
Going first once the talk dies down.

284
00:15:58,540 --> 00:16:01,930
Each one costs you on a day nobody says
thank you.

285
00:16:02,400 --> 00:16:05,350
Drop the fourth and they quit when the pay
quits.

286
00:16:05,720 --> 00:16:09,670
Keep it, and the reason to continue
doesn't ask who's watching.

287
00:16:11,500 --> 00:16:14,970
This hits a different spot, depending on
the life you're in.

288
00:16:15,460 --> 00:16:18,750
If you're young, a lot of the good goes
unseen.

289
00:16:19,200 --> 00:16:22,010
Your boss may not see it. Home may not.

290
00:16:22,520 --> 00:16:23,730
Do it anyway.

291
00:16:24,280 --> 00:16:26,890
You're building a man nobody has met yet.

292
00:16:27,500 --> 00:16:30,650
If you're single, don't wait on a shared
house.

293
00:16:31,020 --> 00:16:32,970
The hidden work counts now.

294
00:16:33,460 --> 00:16:35,070
An apartment has a door.

295
00:16:35,860 --> 00:16:40,410
If you're a new dad, a lot of the best
work happens at three in the morning.

296
00:16:40,900 --> 00:16:44,290
Nobody awake but you and a very small
critic.

297
00:16:44,960 --> 00:16:48,030
If you're married, she doesn't owe you a
parade.

298
00:16:48,400 --> 00:16:52,270
If she thanks you, take it. Don't build
the house on it.

299
00:16:52,780 --> 00:16:55,350
Love her with the Father keeping the
score.

300
00:16:56,020 --> 00:16:59,150
If you've been hurt, maybe nobody ever saw
you.

301
00:16:59,560 --> 00:17:01,790
Not your dad. Not anybody.

302
00:17:02,440 --> 00:17:03,770
I won't rush past that.

303
00:17:04,300 --> 00:17:07,150
Your Father has never once looked away.

304
00:17:07,600 --> 00:17:09,590
You were never invisible to him.

305
00:17:10,640 --> 00:17:15,610
If you're older, and the world has quit
handing you credit, that may be the best

306
00:17:15,560 --> 00:17:17,190
news in a long while.

307
00:17:17,700 --> 00:17:21,210
You get to work for the only one who was
really paying.

308
00:17:21,720 --> 00:17:24,410
A younger man is watching how you carry
that.

309
00:17:24,900 --> 00:17:27,650
If the job is paying well, be careful.

310
00:17:28,100 --> 00:17:31,190
That pay can turn into the only pay you
want.

311
00:17:31,740 --> 00:17:35,010
A strong year can still leave a man
unfaithful.

312
00:17:35,620 --> 00:17:40,110
I've known both in one life, and I've
known a man with the paycheck alone.

313
00:17:40,560 --> 00:17:43,070
He was the loneliest one at the cookout.

314
00:17:44,900 --> 00:17:50,090
Let me land it. A steadfast man works for
God's pay, not the room's.

315
00:17:51,760 --> 00:17:55,870
God exists, and he rewards. He sees behind
the door.

316
00:17:56,320 --> 00:17:59,170
The harvest comes at his time, if you
don't quit.

317
00:18:00,200 --> 00:18:02,310
Don't leave guilty for wanting thanks.

318
00:18:02,840 --> 00:18:05,410
I wanted it. I hovered over a desk.

319
00:18:05,780 --> 00:18:07,470
Send the bill to another place.

320
00:18:07,980 --> 00:18:10,490
The late night. The early drive.

321
00:18:10,800 --> 00:18:13,370
The dishes. The good nobody saw.

322
00:18:13,960 --> 00:18:17,010
None of that was wasted. None of it went
unseen.

323
00:18:17,480 --> 00:18:20,590
Lonnie was right. Somebody is keeping the
record.

324
00:18:20,980 --> 00:18:24,710
Better than a program, and better than a
toast the day you retire.

325
00:18:26,560 --> 00:18:29,210
Open the workbook to Lesson Three.

326
00:18:29,060 --> 00:18:31,570
Rough is fine. True beats pretty.

327
00:18:32,180 --> 00:18:36,370
Three questions. Pause on the question and
put the true answer down.

328
00:18:36,940 --> 00:18:41,710
First. Which of the four marks is faintest
on you right now, and why?

329
00:18:42,200 --> 00:18:46,030
A full sentence. Skip the sentence that
makes you sound humble.

330
00:18:46,340 --> 00:18:47,590
Write the one that fits.

331
00:18:48,080 --> 00:18:50,610
If you landed on the fourth one with me,
good.

332
00:18:50,900 --> 00:18:51,970
It's a big table.

333
00:18:54,320 --> 00:18:54,950
Second.

334
00:18:55,500 --> 00:19:00,710
Where do those four have to work, on an
actual person, or they're only a hobby?

335
00:19:01,380 --> 00:19:03,970
Give that person a name. A face.

336
00:19:04,380 --> 00:19:08,990
Your wife. Your roommate. The person at
work who gets under your skin.

337
00:19:09,580 --> 00:19:14,830
If the four never have to survive a real
human being, you don't have a life under

338
00:19:14,720 --> 00:19:17,050
them. You have a line you like.

339
00:19:19,420 --> 00:19:19,950
Third.

340
00:19:20,420 --> 00:19:25,230
If a man you trust named a different faint
mark on you, would you look again?

341
00:19:25,940 --> 00:19:26,850
Let him finish.

342
00:19:27,320 --> 00:19:30,650
Then answer. Doing this alone still
counts.

343
00:19:30,960 --> 00:19:34,610
You still name it here. You still tell one
man.

344
00:19:36,960 --> 00:19:39,830
Write the four on a card, in your own
hand.

345
00:19:40,180 --> 00:19:41,690
They're in the front of the workbook.

346
00:19:42,180 --> 00:19:44,930
Visor. Mirror. The dash.

347
00:19:45,480 --> 00:19:48,270
Somewhere a plain morning is going to find
it.

348
00:19:49,320 --> 00:19:54,170
If you still owe the last conversation,
say the orders and trade the sentence.

349
00:19:54,500 --> 00:19:57,330
Go have it. This move runs the other way.

350
00:19:57,660 --> 00:19:59,890
The good thing stays unnamed.

351
00:20:00,920 --> 00:20:02,630
So here's your one move.

352
00:20:03,280 --> 00:20:07,590
One. Find the mark that's faintest on you,
and hold that one.

353
00:20:07,920 --> 00:20:13,070
If it's the fourth, do one good thing
before the day is out, and tell nobody.

354
00:20:13,440 --> 00:20:16,370
Not your wife. Not a friend. Not me.

355
00:20:16,860 --> 00:20:19,450
Not at supper. Not in the group text.

356
00:20:19,780 --> 00:20:21,690
You can tell him which mark is thin.

357
00:20:22,060 --> 00:20:26,070
Don't tell him the deed. It will feel like
leaving money on the table.

358
00:20:26,440 --> 00:20:30,130
Leave it. The pay sits where nothing can
spend it early.

359
00:20:31,200 --> 00:20:34,170
One line for your handwriting. Nobody saw
it.

360
00:20:34,480 --> 00:20:37,030
My Father did. Write that once.

361
00:20:39,400 --> 00:20:42,570
Quit fishing for the receipt. Close the
door.

362
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:46,510
Do the good. Let your Father be the one
who pays you.

363
00:20:47,580 --> 00:20:50,750
Next lesson we look at the first mark, on
its own.

364
00:20:51,400 --> 00:20:52,490
Stop Waiting.

365
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:56,610
Go do the hidden good, and give your
Father something to see.
